+ # Lore: Hags and Wretches
+ #lore/creatures #lore/cosmology #status/draft
+ 
+ > "Hags always agree. That's the whole trouble with them." — Morgran "Fin" Deep-Draught, to a client who asked
+ 
+ ---
+ 
+ ## 🌿 Hags
+ 
+ ### What a hag is
+ A hag is one of the **Slack-Born** — god-essence that pooled in a still place and quickened, rather than rising to the High Reach or sinking to the Undertow (see [[Cosmology - The Great Fracture]]). She is humanoid where most fey are not, long-lived past any mortal reckoning, and **rooted to the place she quickened in**. A hag does not travel. She is found, which is the first price of dealing with her.
+ 
+ She is also the only fey that trades. The rest of the Slack-Born are beautiful, unhurried and indifferent to mortal purposes. A hag is interested, which is worse.
+ 
+ ### Where they root
+ Every hag's ground is **still water, or was**. The coast names four kinds, by the water that made them:
+ 
+ - **Bog-Hags** — the fen-mothers of the [[Silted Marshes]], down the Grey Veins where the channels stop moving. Leeches in the hair, peat under the nails. The marsh-folk know roughly where each one is and steer their skiffs wide.
+ - **Brine-Hags** — in sheltered salt water: lagoons, tide-pools, the calm reefs behind the Shield Atolls of [[The Inner Sea]]. Never the open ocean; nothing quickens in water that moves. Sailors' stories make them prettier than they are.
+ - **Briar-Hags** — in drowned woodland, where old forest has gone under at a marsh's edge and the trees stand dead in still water. Barefoot at the treeline, holding a lantern the wrong colour, and lovely.
+ - **Mirage-Hags** — what stayed behind when the water left. A hag is rooted, so when her tarn dried to a salt pan she could not follow it; she is still there, beautiful, offering a cup at a well that is not there. She is always thirsty, and she takes it out on whoever comes.
+ 
+ ### The face she wears
+ The fey are beautiful. Hags were too, once. A hag's trade is working Undertow-touched shards into living flesh, and over centuries she has done it to herself as much as to anyone — the Undertow's pull shows in her the way it shows in the people she changes. The Bog-Hags and Brine-Hags wear it openly. The Briar-Hags and Mirage-Hags wear a **glamour** over it, and the glamour holds until it doesn't: a blade of cold iron, a real wound, or the moment she stops bothering. Marsh-folk say the ugly ones are the honest ones. Marsh-folk have not met many of either.
+ 
+ ### The trade
+ A hag's stock is **change**, and a hag **always agrees**. Bring her a shard and tell her what you want to become, and she will make you into it — gills, bark for skin, eyes that drink the dark. [[Morgran the Abomination|Morgran]] is the coast's best-known customer.
+ 
+ What she will not do is supply the shard. That has to be brought, which is why the [[The Cobalt Feather Syndicate|Cobalt Feather]]'s shard trade has buyers in places with no alchemists in them.
+ 
+ **The price is always paid in full.** What she asks for is rarely coin:
+ - **Years** off the far end of a life.
+ - **A memory** — one specific face, song or road home, gone for good.
+ - **A sense** — taste, smell, colour, or a reflection.
+ - **A service**, named on the spot and done before the season turns.
+ - **A name.** People who knew you remember someone. Not you.
+ - **Something not yet yours** — the next thing you love, the next fortune, the first child.
+ 
+ A hag keeps the **letter** of what she agrees to, exactly, and expects the same. Refuse the price, or leave the service undone, and the work stays where she put it — she simply takes something else instead, and does not haggle a second time.
+ 
+ Anyone she has worked on is a little fey afterwards. Cold iron bites them now, and so does anything else that hunts the fey.
+ 
+ ### Covens
+ Where more than one hag is rooted to the same water, they form a **coven**. The eldest is its mother, and the others are hers the way her teeth are hers: a coven-mother can pass a death-wound onto a sister standing near her, and the coven bleeds so that she does not. Cold iron cuts that thread.
+ 
+ ### Bargaining for her life
+ A hag beaten to her knees does not beg; she **names her price**. A true answer, a curse lifted, safe passage through her ground. She keeps the letter of it — and whoever accepts is bound by its letter in turn.
+ 
+ ### Who hunts them
+ - **The Tidespoken Clergy** hold that anything shaped from an Undertow-shard belongs to the Undertow, and want to burn Morgran for it. A clergy that wants to burn the made has every reason to want the maker; that the hags are rooted in marsh and reef, where Tidespoken reach is thinnest, may be the only reason they still stand.
+ - **Marsh-guides** carry a cold-iron nail on a cord and do not explain why.
+ 
+ ---
+ 
+ ## 💀 Wretches
+ 
+ ### What a Wretch is
+ A Wretch is **what a place keeps** after it has killed someone. Not a curse laid on a corpse by anyone in particular — the place itself, refusing to let go. The Undertow, the sailors say, doesn't drag you down all at once; it just never lets go once it has you. Where Undertow-touched shards lie buried in the deep mud, the cold or the sand, that is literal.
+ 
+ A Wretch does not remember being a person. It remembers **being cold, or drowning, or thirsty**, and it walks toward whatever is warm and alive to make the feeling stop.
+ 
+ ### The three the coast knows
+ - **Bog-Wretches** — the drowned of the [[Silted Marshes]] and of anywhere else freezing water closes over someone: flooded sluices, tidal mud. Waterlogged, slow, and always in threes and fours.
+ - **Grave-Wretches** — the frozen dead of the high passes and the barrows, where the winds come down off [[The Jagged Spine]] in the Winter Moons. Frost-cracked; worst in a blizzard.
+ - **Dust-Wretches** — the dead of thirst and heat: dry tombs, salt pans, dead ground. Sun-split and brittle, and they go up like kindling — the one mercy of meeting them.
+ 
+ Each is untroubled by the very thing that killed it. A Bog-Wretch does not mind the water; a Grave-Wretch does not mind the snow. The living who follow it in do.
+ 
+ ### Wretches and the Lost
+ The **Lost** of the Silted Marshes — travellers and escaped slaves driven feral by the fever and the shards in the mud — are living. A Wretch is what the marsh keeps afterwards. Marsh-folk will tell you that a Lost who dies in the Silt does not always stay down, and that this is the whole reason they burn their own dead on the stilt-platforms rather than sinking them.
+ 
+ ### Wretches and hags
+ Hags do not make Wretches. A Wretch is nobody's work and nobody's purchase; there was no price and no bargain, only a place and a death. But they share ground. A Bog-Hag's fen is full of drowned things, and a hag wastes nothing — where a coven has rooted, its Wretches tend to stand between it and the channel, and nobody has asked the hag whether that is by her design.
+ 
+ ### Laying one down
+ A Wretch that is beaten does not die so much as **gutter out**, the place's grip slipping until there is nothing holding the body up. The grave-keepers of the **Ashen Veil** say that is not enough: a Wretch laid down in the place that kept it will get up again, sooner or later, and the only real mercy is to **carry the body out** and give it to fire or to dry earth somewhere the place cannot reach. It is heavy, filthy, unpaid work. The Veil does it anyway.
+ 
+ > **The Mote:** In Fenmouth, when the marsh gives back someone the village lost, the guides draw lots for who carries them out. Morgran never draws. He just takes the body into his skiff, and he has never once asked who it was.
+ 
+ ---
+ 
+ ## ❓ Open questions — TheTao's call
+ - **Where the Mirage-Hags are.** No desert exists in canon. Candidates: dried tarns where the [[The Twelgorn Kingdom|Twelgorn]] marsh-fringe rises onto dry steppe; the salt flats near Saltmere ([[The Five Duchies of the Whispering Coast|Five Duchies]]), though those pans are worked and peopled.
+ - **Where the Briar-Hags are.** The drowned edge of an old forest — the rotting marsh-timber forests of the Silted Marshes, or the flooded margin of the oak forests below the Spine?
+ - **Dust-Wretches and the Corvus Scar.** The Scar is ash-choked dead ground made by a breach into the Undertow (8 A.A.). If Dust-Wretches walk anywhere on the coast, the dead of the Ash-Blight are the obvious candidates — but that is a large claim about a canon disaster.
+ - **Do the Tidespoken actively hunt hags?** Inferred from Morgran, not stated anywhere.
+ - **Morgran's hag.** Is she still in the Grey Veins, and does she remember him?
+ - **Cold iron.** The vault has never said why it answers the fey. Folk knowledge, or something about how god-essence and pure iron meet?
+ 
+ ---
